QUESTIONS=[
 'In each case, find the tangent and normal equations: (a) at (1,2) to x²+y² = 5; (b) at (−3,−2) to x²+y² = 13; (c) at (4,1) to x²+y²−4x+2y−3 = 0.',
 'In each case, find the tangent and normal equations: (a) at (cos60°,sin60°) to 36(x²+y²) = 13; (b) at (2cos45°,2sin45°) to x²+y² = 4; (c) at (cos30°,sin30°) to x²+y² = 1.',
 'For what value of n: (a) lx+my+n = 0 touches x²+y² = a²? (b) x+y+n = 0 touches x²+y² = 9? (c) 2x+2y+n = 0 touches x²+y² = 81?',
 'For what value of c: (a) y = mx+c touches x²+y² = a²? (b) y = −x+c touches x²+y² = 9? (c) y = −x−c/2 touches x²+y² = 81?',
 'Find the condition at which lx+my+n = 0 touches x²+y²+2gx+2fy+c = 0.',
 'For what value of n: (a) 3x+4y+n = 0 touches x²+y²−4x−6y−12 = 0? (b) x−2y+n = 0 touches x²+y²+3x+6y−5 = 0? (c) 2x+y+n = 0 touches x²+y²−2x−10y+21 = 0?',
 'If the tangent length from point P to x²+y² = a² equals the perpendicular distance from P to lx+my+n = 0, find the locus of P.',
 'Find the locus of point P: (a) if the length of the tangent from P to x²+y² = 9 equals the perpendicular distance from P to 3x+4y+3 = 0; (b) if the length of the tangent from P to x²+y² = 25 equals the perpendicular distance from P to 4x+3y+3 = 0.',
 ' (a) The tangent length from (f,g) to x²+y² = 6 is twice the tangent length to x²+y²+3x+3y = 0. Prove that f²+g²+4f+4g+2 = 0. (b) The tangent length from (f,g) to x²+y² = 4 is 4 times the tangent length to x²+y²+2x+2y = 0. Prove that 15f²+15g²+32f+32g+4 = 0.',
 'Find the equations of the tangents to (a) x²+y² = 4 parallel to x+2y+3 = 0; (b) x²+y² = 25 parallel to 3x+4y+3 = 0.',
 'Prove that the lines (a) x = 8 and y = 7 touch x²+y²−6x−4y−12 = 0, and find their contact points; (b) x+y−1 = 0 and x−y+1 = 0 touch x²+y²−4x−2y+3 = 0, and find their contact points.',
 'Find the equations of tangents (a) to x²+y² = 2 making an angle 45° with the x-axis; (b) to 3x²+3y² = 1 making an angle 30° with the x-axis; (c) to x²+y² = 4 making an angle 60° with the x-axis.'
]
STEPS=[
 ['Check that each point lies on its given circle before applying the tangent formula.','(a) 1²+2² = 5. At (1,2) the tangent is x+2y = 5 and the radial normal is 2x−y = 0.','(b) (−3)²+(−2)² = 13. The tangent is −3x−2y = 13; the normal is −2x+3y = 0.','(c) Complete squares: (x−2)²+(y+1)² = 8, giving center (2,−1).','The radius to (4,1) is (2,2), with squared length 8. The tangent is 2(x−4)+2(y−1) = 0, or x+y−5 = 0.','The normal through (2,−1) and (4,1) is x−y−3 = 0. Every tangent and normal passes through its stated point, and each tangent direction is perpendicular to its radius.'],
 ['Use the exact degree-angle values and verify circle membership first.','(a) The point is (1/2,√3/2) and has squared norm 1. Substitution into 36(x²+y²) = 13 gives 36 ≠ 13.','Thus there is no tangent or normal at that point on the stated circle. The center-to-point distance 1 exceeds the actual radius √13/6, but tangents from an external point are a different request.','If the apparent intended circle were x²+y² = 1, its tangent would be x+√3y = 2 and normal √3x−y = 0; these are explicitly conditional, not answers to the literal question.','(b) The point is (√2,√2), with squared norm 4. The tangent is √2x+√2y = 4, or x+y = 2√2. The normal is x−y = 0.','(c) The point is (√3/2,1/2), with squared norm 1. The tangent is √3x+y = 2 and normal x−√3y = 0.','Both valid pairs pass through the contact, and the tangent normal is the nonzero radius vector.'],
 ['A line lx+my+n = 0 has distance |n|/√(l²+m²) from the origin when l²+m² > 0.','For a circle with radius a > 0, equate that distance to a.','(a) |n| = a√(l²+m²), hence n = ±a√(l²+m²).','(b) The normal is (1,1) and radius is 3, giving n = ±3√2.','(c) The normal is (2,2), whose length is 2√2, and radius is 9, giving n = ±18√2.','Each sign gives a distinct tangent on the corresponding side of the circle. If a is not prescribed positive, the geometric radius is |a|; a = 0 is degenerate.'],
 ['For y = mx+c, the center-to-line distance is |c|/√(1+m²).','(a) Set it equal to radius a > 0, giving c = ±a√(1+m²).','(b) With m = −1 and radius 3, obtain c = ±3√2.','(c) The line has slope −1 and y-intercept −c/2.','Its distance is |c|/(2√2), so equality to radius 9 gives |c| = 18√2.','Thus c = ±18√2. Both signs satisfy the original distance condition; the sign of the parameter is opposite to the sign of the intercept.'],
 ['Complete squares: the circle center is C(−g,−f), with radius R = √(g²+f²−c).','Require R² > 0 and l²+m² > 0 for a genuine circle and line.','Evaluate the line at the center: l(−g)+m(−f)+n = n−lg−mf.','The perpendicular distance from C to the line is |n−lg−mf|/√(l²+m²).','Equate this distance to R and square both nonnegative sides.','The required condition is (n−lg−mf)² = (l²+m²)(g²+f²−c). Equivalently n = lg+mf±R√(l²+m²).'],
 ['For each circle, calculate its center and radius, then set the distance from the center to the line equal to the radius.','(a) Completing squares gives center (2,3) and radius 5.','The line value at the center is 18+n and its normal length is 5. Thus |18+n| = 25, giving n = 7 or −43.','(b) The center is (−3/2,−3) and squared radius is 65/4. The line value is n+9/2 and normal length is √5.','Thus |n+9/2| = √325/2 = 5√13/2, giving n = (−9±5√13)/2.','(c) The center is (1,5) and squared radius is 5. The line value is n+7 and normal length is √5.','Thus |n+7| = 5, giving n = −2 or −12. All values satisfy the original distance equality.'],
 ['Let P = (x,y), with x²+y² > a² for an external point and a > 0.','Its tangent length is √(x²+y²−a²).','Its distance to the given line is |lx+my+n|/√(l²+m²), with l²+m² > 0.','Equate these nonnegative quantities and square: (l²+m²)(x²+y²−a²) = (lx+my+n)².','Expanded, this is m²x²+l²y²−2lmxy−2lnx−2mny−n²−a²(l²+m²) = 0.','Restrict this equation to x²+y² > a² for genuine external tangent segments. If zero-length contact is allowed, replace > with ≥; no interior point can satisfy the real unsquared length condition.'],
 ['Use the general locus equation (l²+m²)(x²+y²−R²) = (lx+my+n)².','(a) Substitute l = 3, m = 4, n = 3, R = 3: 25(x²+y²−9) = (3x+4y+3)².','Expand and collect to obtain 16x²+9y²−24xy−18x−24y−234 = 0.','Take the external portion x²+y² > 9 for positive tangent length. On the squared equation, nonnegativity excludes interior points automatically.','(b) Substitute l = 4, m = 3, n = 3, R = 5: 25(x²+y²−25) = (4x+3y+3)².','Expand to 9x²+16y²−24xy−24x−18y−634 = 0.','Take x²+y² > 25 for a genuine external tangent. Both equations are equivalent to the original nonnegative distance equality on their stated domains.'],
 ['Let S = f²+g². Tangent length squared is the normalized circle polynomial evaluated at (f,g).','(a) The first squared length is S−6 and the second is S+3f+3g.','A factor of 2 in length becomes 4 after squaring: S−6 = 4(S+3f+3g).','Rearrange to 3S+12f+12g+6 = 0 and divide by 3: f²+g²+4f+4g+2 = 0.','For real tangent segments require S ≥ 6; if both points must be strictly external require S > 6. Under the derived equation the second squared length equals (S−6)/4.','(b) The squared lengths are S−4 and S+2f+2g. A factor of 4 in length becomes 16: S−4 = 16(S+2f+2g).','Rearrange to 15S+32f+32g+4 = 0, exactly the requested equation.','Require S ≥ 4, or S > 4 for strictly external points; the second squared length then equals (S−4)/16. These restrictions prevent including parts of the algebraic locus with imaginary tangent lengths.'],
 ['Parallel lines share the same nonzero normal, while the new constant is chosen for tangency.','(a) Write x+2y+d = 0. Its origin distance is |d|/√5.','Equate to radius 2: d = ±2√5, giving x+2y±2√5 = 0.','(b) Write 3x+4y+d = 0. Its origin distance is |d|/5.','Equate to radius 5: d = ±25, giving 3x+4y±25 = 0.','Each tangent is parallel to its stated reference line and has center-to-line distance exactly equal to the circle radius.'],
 ['(a) Complete squares: (x−3)²+(y−2)² = 25, giving center (3,2), radius 5.','The distance from this center to x = 8 is 5; the perpendicular foot (8,2) lies on the circle, proving tangency and giving its contact.','The distance to y = 7 is also 5; the contact is (3,7).','(b) Complete squares: (x−2)²+(y−1)² = 2, giving center (2,1), radius √2.','At the center, x+y−1 = 2 and x−y+1 = 2. Each normal has length √2, so both line distances are √2.','Project (2,1) onto the first line: subtract (2/2)(1,1), giving contact (1,0).','Project onto the second: subtract (2/2)(1,−1), giving contact (1,2). Each point satisfies its line and circle, verifying both contacts.'],
 ['Use the standard inclination convention m = tanθ and tangent intercept c = ±R√(1+m²).','(a) R = √2 and θ = 45° gives m = 1, c = ±2, hence y = x±2.','(b) Divide by 3 to get radius R = 1/√3. With θ = 30°, m = 1/√3.','Then c = ±(1/√3)√(4/3) = ±2/3, giving y = x/√3±2/3.','(c) R = 2 and θ = 60° gives m = √3 and c = ±4, hence y = √3x±4.','Their origin distances equal the respective radii. The listed angles are the conventional inclinations measured counterclockwise from the positive x-axis; an unoriented acute-angle interpretation would also allow negative slopes.']
]
ANSWERS=[
 '(a) tangent x+2y = 5, normal 2x−y = 0; (b) tangent −3x−2y = 13, normal −2x+3y = 0; (c) tangent x+y−5 = 0, normal x−y−3 = 0.',
 '(a) invalid contact as printed; (b) tangent x+y = 2√2, normal x−y = 0; (c) tangent √3x+y = 2, normal x−√3y = 0.',
 '(a) n = ±a√(l²+m²); (b) n = ±3√2; (c) n = ±18√2.',
 '(a) c = ±a√(1+m²); (b) c = ±3√2; (c) c = ±18√2.',
 '(n−lg−mf)² = (l²+m²)(g²+f²−c), with positive squared radius and nonzero line normal.',
 '(a) n = 7 or −43; (b) n = (−9±5√13)/2; (c) n = −2 or −12.',
 '(l²+m²)(x²+y²−a²) = (lx+my+n)², with x²+y² > a² for external points.',
 '(a) 16x²+9y²−24xy−18x−24y−234 = 0; (b) 9x²+16y²−24xy−24x−18y−634 = 0, on the external domains.',
 '(a) f²+g²+4f+4g+2 = 0 with f²+g² ≥ 6; (b) 15f²+15g²+32f+32g+4 = 0 with f²+g² ≥ 4; use strict inequalities for strictly external points.',
 '(a) x+2y±2√5 = 0; (b) 3x+4y±25 = 0.',
 '(a) contacts (8,2) and (3,7); (b) contacts (1,0) and (1,2).',
 '(a) y = x±2; (b) y = x/√3±2/3; (c) y = √3x±4, under the standard inclination convention.'
]
PAGES=[page('120_page_1.jpg',231,[heading('Exercise 8.2')]+[paragraph(str(i)+'. '+q) for i,q in enumerate(QUESTIONS,1)]+[note('Question 2(a) is invalid as printed because its stated point is not on 36(x²+y²) = 13. The learning solution retains that equation and marks the apparent unit-circle interpretation as conditional. Tangent-length loci require real external-point domains; inclination questions use the conventional angle from the positive x-axis.')])]
